---
// English Sample Post
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const post = {
  title: 'Getting Started with FEA: From Theory to ANSYS Practice',
  date: '2026-06-27',
  category: 'Software Tutorial',
  tags: ['ANSYS', 'FEA', 'Beginner'],
};

const formatDate = (dateStr: string) => {
  return new Date(dateStr).toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric' });
};
---

<BaseLayout title={post.title} lang="en">
  <article class="article container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <div class="meta">
        <time>{formatDate(post.date)}</time>
        <span class="meta-separator"></span>
        <span class="tag tag-category">{post.category}</span>
      </div>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <h2>What is Finite Element Analysis?</h2>
      <p>
        Finite Element Analysis (FEA) is a numerical method that discretizes a complex continuous body into a finite number
        of simple elements. By establishing approximate equations for each element and assembling them into a global system,
        FEA computes structural responses such as displacement, stress, and strain at each node.
      </p>
      <p>
        The core idea is <strong>"divide and conquer"</strong> — break an unsolvable complex problem into countless solvable
        small problems, then combine their solutions for an overall approximation.
      </p>

      <h2>Why Learn FEA?</h2>
      <p>
        In structural engineering, mechanical design, and aerospace, many real-world problems involve complex geometries and
        loading conditions that cannot be solved analytically. FEA provides a systematic numerical approach to these problems.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        Learning FEA is not just about mastering software — it's about understanding the mathematical principles
        and physical meaning behind it. Only then can you judge whether the software results are reasonable.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Hands-on: Simply Supported Beam in ANSYS Workbench</h2>

      <h3>Problem Description</h3>
      <p>
        A simply supported beam with length L = 1 m, rectangular cross-section 50 mm × 100 mm, structural steel
        (E = 200 GPa, ν = 0.3), with a concentrated force F = 1000 N at midspan. Find the maximum deflection and stress.
      </p>

      <h3>Analysis Steps</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Create Geometry</strong>: Build the 3D solid model in DesignModeler or SpaceClaim</li>
        <li><strong>Define Material</strong>: Add structural steel in Engineering Data</li>
        <li><strong>Mesh</strong>: Use default mesh settings, check mesh quality</li>
        <li><strong>Apply Loads & BCs</strong>: Simple supports at both ends, concentrated force at midspan</li>
        <li><strong>Solve</strong>: Run the solver</li>
        <li><strong>Post-process</strong>: View displacement and stress contours</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Result Verification</h3>
      <pre><code>Max deflection (analytical): δ = FL³ / (48EI)
Max stress (analytical): σ = FL / (4W)

Where:
  I = bh³/12 = 50 × 100³ / 12 = 4.167 × 10⁶ mm⁴
  W = bh²/6 = 50 × 100² / 6 = 83,333 mm³</code></pre>
      <p>
        Compare FEA results with analytical solutions — the error should be within 5%.
        If the error is larger, check mesh density, boundary conditions, and load application.
      </p>

      <h2>Summary</h2>
      <p>
        This article introduced the basic idea of FEA and demonstrated a static analysis workflow in ANSYS Workbench.
        Key takeaways: understand the "discretization" concept; master the full pre-processing to post-processing pipeline;
        always verify your results.
      </p>
    </div>

    <footer class="article-footer">
      <div class="tags">
        {post.tags.map((tag) => (
          <span class="tag">{tag}</span>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="article-nav">
        <a href="/en/blog" class="btn">← Back to Articles</a>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article { max-width: var(--max-width); }
  .article-header { margin-bottom: var(--space-2xl); padding-bottom: var(--space-xl); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .article-header .meta { margin-bottom: var(--space-md); }
  .article-header h1 { font-size: 2.25rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.3; }
  .article-body h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .article-footer { margin-top: var(--space-3xl); padding-top: var(--space-xl); border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  .tags { margin-bottom: var(--space-lg); }
  .article-nav { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; }
  @media (max-width: 768px) { .article-header h1 { font-size: 1.75rem; } }
</style>
